<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Istakhr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Stakhr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Istakhr was an ancient city in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>, a few kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>. After the Achaemenid period it became the main city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a>, and in the early 3rd century CE it was the home of the Sasanian family, whose temple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anahita">Anahita</a> stood there. It remained the chief city of Fars until the Arab conquest of the 7th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiraz">Shiraz</a> replaced it, and was later abandoned.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/istakhr.jpg" alt="Istakhr" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: درفش کاویانی (Kaviani flag) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Estakhr_Darafsh_(55).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Istakhr stood in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fars">Fars</a>, about 5 km north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a>, in the valley of the Polvar River between the Kuh-e Rahmat and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naqsh-e-rostam">Naqsh-e Rostam</a>, where the valley opens onto the plain of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marvdasht">Marvdasht</a>. The name is the New Persian form of the Middle Persian Stakhr, believed to mean stronghold. Istakhr was probably first part of the settlements around the Achaemenid royal residences, and became a separate city soon after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> brought down Persepolis; much of Persepolis's rubble seems to have been used to build it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seleucus-i-nicator">Seleucus I</a> died in 280 BCE, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persis">Persis</a> began to reassert their independence, and Istakhr, better protected by its hills than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persepolis">Persepolis</a> and on the road to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isfahan">Isfahan</a>, appears to have been the centre of resistance. It flourished as the capital of the Frataraka governors and Kings of Persis until the early 3rd century CE. Sasan, ancestor of the Sasanian dynasty, came from Istakhr and was warden of its Anahid fire-temple. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papak">Papak</a> dethroned the local ruler Gochihr in about 205, and Papak's sons Shapur and Ardashir were the last two Kings of Persis.</p><p>In 224 Ardashir founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a>, and Istakhr briefly served as its first capital, then as the principal city and religious centre of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pars">Pars</a>. It held an important mint and the royal treasury, which kept one of the limited copies of the Great Avesta.</p><p>Istakhr resisted the Arab conquest fiercely. A campaign in 640 failed, the city surrendered in 643 and then revolted, and further campaigns in 648 or 649 and 649 caused the death of many inhabitants.</p><p>It remained a stronghold of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> and a fairly important place in the early Islamic period, and its mint struck Umayyad and Abbasid coins. On 11 April 890 Amr ibn al-Layth defeated a caliphal governor there. The Buyid Abu Kalijar later sent an army under Qutulmish that destroyed and pillaged the city, which never recovered and became a village of no more than a hundred inhabitants. Its castle held out until Shah Abbas besieged it, and Pietro della Valle saw it in ruins in 1621.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Eugène Flandin and Pascal Coste made the most detailed account of the ruins before the 20th century, in late 1840. Ernst Herzfeld and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-chicago">University of Chicago</a> team led by Erich Schmidt explored the site only cursorily in the first half of the 20th century, and Sasanian Istakhr remains largely unexcavated.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Istakhr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Ardashir's family traced its descent from Sasan, traditionally a priest at the temple of Anahita at <b>Istakhr</b>, from whom the dynasty took its name.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Alexander's conquest Persis became part of the Seleucid Empire, but in the 3rd century BCE local dynasts called frataraka ('governor' or 'leader') began issuing their own coins from <b>Istakhr</b> and Persepolis.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-persis">Kingdom of Persis</a></li></ul></section>
